# WHY THE SITE WAS SLOW.
#
# Every .js, .css and .html here was served "no-cache", which does not mean "do not store" - it
# means the browser must ASK the server every single time before it may use what it already has.
# nexus-core.js is 2.1MB of source (600KB over the wire after brotli). Every page in JAIN-E is a
# separate HTML file that loads it again, so moving from Bookings to Invoices to CRM meant a
# round trip to Netlify and a re-parse of 2.1MB of JavaScript, on every move. Measured at 3.2
# seconds for that one file.
#
# The app already solves this properly: every script tag carries ?v=<number>, and that number is
# bumped on every change. A URL that changes when its contents change can be cached FOREVER -
# that is the entire point of versioning it. "no-cache" threw that away and made the ?v= do
# nothing but add characters.
#
# So: the HTML is still no-cache, because the HTML is what carries the new ?v= and it must be
# picked up the moment it ships. Everything the HTML points at is immutable, because its URL
# changes whenever it does.
#
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE ONE RULE THIS DEPENDS ON: bump ?v= in the HTML whenever a .js or .css file changes.
# Ship a change without bumping it and browsers will keep the old file for a year. Every page's
# script tags are stamped together, so bumping means a search and replace across *.html, not
# remembering which page uses what.
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/*.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Images are not versioned, so they get a day rather than a year: long enough to stop them being
# re-fetched on every page, short enough that replacing a logo reaches people the same week.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
